UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 39, Lesson 2: Healthy habits challenge', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates consistency, not perfection.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nDesign four habits for a week challenge and a kindness rule (no body talk).\n\nFEEDBACK\nAny four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule.\n\nWATCH FOR\nChallenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRun a 7-day habits challenge. Reflect wins and one restart strategy.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates consistency, not perfection.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Run a 7-day habits challenge. Reflect wins and one restart strategy. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule. Reasoning: I used habit by attending to challenge rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful. Correction: Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule. Why: the correction restores accurate use of habit.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating habit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with habit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule. This matches the lesson skill for habit.","options":["Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule."],"question":"Design four habits for a week challenge and a kindness rule (no body talk). Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule.","Careful use of challenge with clear evidence.","Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful.","A healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates consistency, not perfection."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named challenge, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the challenge used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"A learner is practising habit. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule. uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about habit.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example 7-day card with four habits; miss one ≠ fail — restart next day.","Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful.","Run a 7-day habits challenge. Reflect wins and one restart strategy.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating habit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule. Reasoning: I used habit by attending to challenge rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with habit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful. Correction: Any four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule. Why: the correction restores accurate use of habit.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y4-w39-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise habit.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use habit with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify challenge and explain why it matters.","I can practise track independently on a new prompt.","I can notice celebrate needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates consistency, not perfection. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","A healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates consistency, not perfection."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample 7-day card with four habits; miss one ≠ fail — restart next day.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDesign four habits for a week challenge and a kindness rule (no body talk).\n\nFEEDBACK\nAny four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule.","explain":"A healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates consistency, not perfection.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Habits challenge.","caption":"No body ranking.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Daily habits</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">small actions</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Celebrate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">consistency</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example 7-day card with four habits; miss one ≠ fail — restart next day. Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example 7-day card with four habits; miss one ≠ fail — restart next day. Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample 7-day card with four habits; miss one ≠ fail — restart next day. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Week challenge.","caption":"Kindness rule.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">7-day card</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">restart</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">thin/fast contest</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChallenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful. Here is a more helpful example: Example 7-day card with four habits; miss one ≠ fail — restart next day.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAny four supportive habits + no body-shaming rule.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Challenges that rank thinnest/fastest are harmful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA healthy habits challenge tracks small daily actions (water, move, sleep routine, kind act) and celebrates consistency, not perfection. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 39, Lesson 2: Healthy habits challenge","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy active lifestyles","week":39,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample 7-day card with four habits; miss one ≠ fail — restart next day.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Habits challenge.","caption":"No body ranking.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Daily habits</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">small actions</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Celebrate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">consistency</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Week challenge.","caption":"Kindness rule.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\